      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the soy sauces, sugar, garlic, and sesame oil. Add the beef cubes and toss to coat. Marinate for at least 30 minutes, ideally 2 hours."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the dipping sauce by mixing lime juice with a generous pinch of freshly ground black pepper and a tiny pinch of seasoning. Set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Arrange sliced cucumber and tomato wedges on a serving platter. Set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the wok over the highest heat until smoking. Add the oil and heat for 30 seconds."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the beef in a single layer. Leave for 1 minute without touching. Then shake and toss the wok continuously for 2 to 3 minutes for medium-rare beef."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the onion wedges in the last minute of cooking. Toss together."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove from heat and add the butter. Toss until melted and glossy. Serve immediately on the platter with the dipping sauce."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The beef is grey and steamed rather than seared and browned.",
              "cause": "The wok was not hot enough or too many pieces were cooked at once.",
              "fix": "Heat the wok until smoking before adding oil. Cook the beef in two batches if needed to keep the pan very hot."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The beef is tough and chewy.",
              "cause": "A tough cut was used or the beef was overcooked.",
              "fix": "Use sirloin or fillet and cook to medium-rare. The beef should still be slightly pink inside after 3 minutes of shaking."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The marinade burns in the pan.",
              "cause": "The sugar in the marinade catches quickly at high heat.",
              "fix": "Pat the beef dry before cooking to remove excess marinade. The sugar should caramelise, not burn."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Bò lúc lắc has its roots in Vietnamese French-influenced cooking of the south. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "It is a popular restaurant dish in Ho Chi Minh City and is served at celebrations and special meals. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The use of butter alongside soy sauce shows the French culinary influence clearly. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "It is often eaten with a fried egg on top at Vietnamese restaurants. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Outside Vietnam, it is one of the most recognised Vietnamese beef dishes."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
